"""
The LU factorization and the solution of linear systems in Float64 over the views of Standard.Array, computed by getrf and gesv of the package MircoBlis through the bindings of Bindings.Lapack.

- lu gives the factors of the LU decomposition with partial pivoting, solve gives the x of a x = b for a matrix of right hand sides, one column each.
- A matrix is read in the row-major order of its view and handed to the routines as a column-major copy; every result comes back as a new owning array in row-major order.
- A rank that is not 2, an element type that is not Float64, a shape that does not fit the operation or a dimension above largestDimension is a requires that fails, and the programme stops there.
- A routine that reports info other than 0 comes back as the Left of the Either with the code of the routine, a success as the Right.  An ensures cannot look into an Either, so the sizes of the two answers are held by the invariants of their records and read through accessors whose ensures state them: the factors are square of the order and the pivots carry one place per row, and x carries as many rows as solveRows answers and as many columns as solveColumns answers.
- The factors hold the lower factor below the diagonal with the unit diagonal not stored, the upper factor on and above it, and one row exchange per row.
- Importing this module chooses the C backend: a programme that does not import it links no library of the package MircoBlis.

    var a: Array := zeros(float64Type(), shape2(2, 2));
    setFloat64(&!a, 0, 4.0);
    setFloat64(&!a, 1, 2.0);
    setFloat64(&!a, 2, 2.0);
    setFloat64(&!a, 3, 3.0);
    case lu(view(&a)) of
        when Right(right as made: LuFactors) do
            destroyLuFactors(made);
        when Left(left as code: Int32) do
            skip;
    end case;
    destroy(a);
"""
pragma Targets(Names => "linux openbsd freebsd darwin");
import Standard.Array (
    Array,
    View,
    float64Type,
    sameShape,
    shape,
    shape1,
    shape2,
    size,
    view,
    viewElementType,
    viewExtent,
    viewRank
);

module Standard.ArrayLu is
    """
    The largest number of rows or columns a matrix of this module may
    have: the routines of the backend take every dimension as a 32-bit
    whole number.
    """
    function largestDimension(): Index
        ensures result = 2147483647;

    """
    The factors of the LU decomposition with partial pivoting: the lower
    factor below the diagonal with the unit diagonal not stored, the
    upper factor on and above it, and the row exchanges of the pivoting.
    Linear: destroyLuFactors gives it back.  The record is opaque; its
    sizes are read through luOrder and luPivotCount, its matrices
    through factorsOf and pivotsOf.  Its invariant holds the sizes: the
    factors are square of the order, and the pivots carry one place per
    row.
    """
    type LuFactors: Linear;

    """
    The x of a solved system, a new owning array in row-major order.
    Linear: destroySolution gives it back.  The record is opaque; its
    sizes are read through solveRows and solveColumns, x itself through
    xOf.  Its invariant holds the shape of x: as many rows as solveRows
    answers and as many columns as solveColumns answers.
    """
    type Solution: Linear;

    """
    Give the factors of the LU decomposition back.
    No contract: it only frees or closes what it is given.
    """
    function destroyLuFactors(given: LuFactors): Unit;

    """
    Give the x of the solved system back.
    No contract: it only frees or closes what it is given.
    """
    function destroySolution(given: Solution): Unit;

    """
    The order of the factored matrix: as many rows and columns as the
    factors carry, and as many places as the pivots carry.
    """
    generic [R: Region]
    function luOrder(made: &[LuFactors, R]): Index
        ensures (result >= 1) and (result <= largestDimension())
        ensures luPivotCount(made) = result;

    """
    The number of the row exchanges of the pivoting, one per row of the
    factored matrix.
    """
    generic [R: Region]
    function luPivotCount(made: &[LuFactors, R]): Index
        ensures (result >= 1) and (result <= largestDimension());

    """
    A new array with the elements of the factored matrix, row major,
    square, of the order luOrder answers.
    """
    generic [R: Region]
    function factorsOf(made: &[LuFactors, R]): Array
        ensures sameShape(shape(&result), shape2(luOrder(made), luOrder(made)));

    """
    A new array with the row exchanges of the pivoting, of as many
    places as luPivotCount answers.
    """
    generic [R: Region]
    function pivotsOf(made: &[LuFactors, R]): Array
        ensures sameShape(shape(&result), shape1(luPivotCount(made)));

    """
    The rows of x: the order of the matrix the call solved.
    """
    generic [R: Region]
    function solveRows(made: &[Solution, R]): Index
        ensures (result >= 1) and (result <= largestDimension());

    """
    The columns of x: the columns of the matrix of right hand sides.
    """
    generic [R: Region]
    function solveColumns(made: &[Solution, R]): Index
        ensures (result >= 1) and (result <= largestDimension());

    """
    A new array with the elements of x, row major, of as many rows as
    solveRows answers and as many columns as solveColumns answers.
    """
    generic [R: Region]
    function xOf(made: &[Solution, R]): Array
        ensures sameShape(shape(&result), shape2(solveRows(made), solveColumns(made)));

    ---
    --- The factorization and the system
    ---

    """
    The factors of the LU decomposition with partial pivoting of the
    square matrix a, through getrf.  The lower factor stands below the
    diagonal with the unit diagonal not stored, the upper factor on and
    above it, and the pivots carry one row exchange per row.  The Left
    of the Either carries the code of the routine when it reports info
    other than 0, a negative one for a refused call and the number of
    the row where the matrix is exactly singular for a positive one;
    the Right carries the factors.  The result is an Either, which an
    ensures cannot look into; the sizes of the factors are held by the
    invariant of LuFactors and read through luOrder and luPivotCount,
    whose ensures state them.
    """
    generic [A: Region]
    function lu(a: View[A]): Either[Int32, LuFactors]
        requires viewElementType(a) = float64Type()
        requires viewRank(a) = 2
        requires viewExtent(a, 0) = viewExtent(a, 1)
        requires viewExtent(a, 0) <= largestDimension();

    """
    The x with a x = b for the square matrix a and the matrix b of right
    hand sides, one column each, through gesv, which factors a with
    partial pivoting and solves.  The Left of the Either carries the
    code of the routine when it reports info other than 0, a negative
    one for a refused call and the number of the row where the matrix is
    exactly singular for a positive one; the Right carries x.  The
    result is an Either, which an ensures cannot look into; the shape of
    x is held by the invariant of Solution and read through solveRows
    and solveColumns, whose ensures state it.
    """
    generic [A: Region, B: Region]
    function solve(a: View[A], b: View[B]): Either[Int32, Solution]
        requires viewElementType(a) = float64Type()
        requires viewElementType(b) = float64Type()
        requires viewRank(a) = 2
        requires viewRank(b) = 2
        requires viewExtent(a, 0) = viewExtent(a, 1)
        requires viewExtent(b, 0) = viewExtent(a, 0)
        requires viewExtent(a, 0) <= largestDimension()
        requires viewExtent(b, 1) <= largestDimension();
end module.
